> If you prefer the original iconic jumbo muffins, see the original recipe. We use the modified values for regular sized muffins for easier portioning.

Preheat the oven to 400°F.

Line two #muffin tins{} with muffin liners. Set aside. 

In a #large mixing bowl{}, whisk together @all-purpose flour{2.25%cup}, @granulated sugar{1%cup}, @granulated sugar{2%tbsp}, @fine sea salt{1%tsp}, @poppy seeds{2%Tbsp}, @baking powder{2%tsp}, and @baking soda{1/2%tsp}.

In another #bowl, whisk together @full fat sour cream{1/2%cup}(heaping, room temperature), @buttermilk{3/4%cup}(room temperature), @vegetable oil{3/4%cup}, @eggs{2%large}(room temperature), @almond extract{2%tsp}, and @vanilla extract{1%tsp} until smooth.

> If you'd like to avoid buying buttermilk, follow ¾ cup sour cream + ¼ cup milk == 1 cup butter cream. Thus replace with a little over half cup sour cream, a little under ¼ cup milk. Conversely, for 1/2 cup of sour cream, use 2/3 cup buttermilk and cut the liquid by a scant 3 tablespoons. You may want to add some softened unsalted butter too if you do this for texture.

Add the two bowls' contents together (wet to dry ideally) and fold until combined. The batter will be somewhat thick. 

> Avoid overmixing the batter. Too much air can cause the muffins to collapse or create tougher muffins.

Spoon or scoop the batter into the prepared muffin pan, filling up almost to the top. 

Bake for ~{5%minutes} at 400°F, and then without taking the muffins out of the oven, reduce the temperature to 350°F and bake for another ~{15%minutes} or until a toothpick inserted in the center comes out clean. 

Cool in the muffin pan for about ~{5-10%minutes}, then remove to a wire rack.

> Room temperature, these keep for 2-3 days in an airtight container. Refrigerated, up to 1 week in an airtight container. Frozen, 2-3 months wrapped tightly in plastic wrap and stored in a freezer-safe bag or container.